{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:lgs-class-and-witness-separation",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "After matching the common selector-gated syntax,\n\\[\n\\Theta_H=\\mathfrak M_{\\mathrm{LGS}}(G^+)\\cap\n\\left\\{\\theta:\\operatorname{supp}_{p_\\theta}(S)=H,\\ p_\\theta(c)>0\\text{ for every }c\\in\\mathcal C_H^{\\mathrm{adm}}(G^+)\\right\\}\n\\subseteq\\mathfrak M_{\\mathrm{LGS}}(G^+).\n\\]\nThe inclusion is strict whenever the published class contains a law violating at least one displayed restriction.  Moreover, \\(\\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}\\subseteq\\Theta_H^2\\subseteq\\mathfrak M_{\\mathrm{LGS}}(G^+)^2\\).  If a pair in \\(\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{LGS}}^{(5)}\\) assigns positive mass outside \\(H\\), has a zero admissible cell, or fails equality on some coordinate of \\(O_H\\), then that pair is not in \\(\\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}\\).  Consequently the published failure relation alone does not imply membership in the fixed-support global-twin relation; if it contains any such pair, \\(\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{LGS}}^{(5)}\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}\\).",
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:exact-selector-support",
      "ass:admissible-cell-interior",
      "def:model-class",
      "def:certificate",
      "thm:certificate-soundness",
      "ass:finite-categorical"
    ],
    "justification": "The inclusion comparison isolates the obstruction to importing a larger-class branchwise witness into the fixed-support completeness theorem.",
    "gap": "The published failure construction does not type every output as an exact-\\(H\\), strictly positive, full-joint-law twin in the restricted-support subclass.",
    "consumer": "Reviewers and implementers can distinguish a legal fixed-support nonidentification certificate from a witness that adds a context or preserves only part of the supplied table.",
    "proof_tex": "Define\n\\[\n \\mathcal R_H=\n \\left\\{\\theta\\in\\Theta(G^+):\n \\operatorname{supp}_{p_\\theta}(S)=H,\\quad\n p_\\theta(c)>0\\ \\text{for every }c\\in\n \\mathcal C_H^{\\mathrm{adm}}(G^+)\\right\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe probabilities in (1) are well typed because \\(\\textnormal{ass:finite-categorical}\\) gives finite alphabets and the recursive SCM semantics defines \\(p_\\theta\\).  By the definition of \\(\\mathfrak M_{\\mathrm{LGS}}(G^+)\\), that class imposes the common recursive selector-gated syntax, independent-root factorization, and canonical response representation before the two restrictions in (1).  By \\(\\textnormal{def:model-class}\\), \\(\\Theta_H\\) imposes exactly that common syntax together with those restrictions.  Therefore\n\\[\n \\Theta_H=\\mathfrak M_{\\mathrm{LGS}}(G^+)\\cap\\mathcal R_H\n \\subseteq\\mathfrak M_{\\mathrm{LGS}}(G^+).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nIf the published class contains a parameter violating either restriction in (1), it belongs to the right-hand class in (2) but not to \\(\\Theta_H\\), which proves the asserted conditional strictness.\n\nBy the declared support-exact global-twin relation,\n\\[\n \\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}\n =\\{(\\theta^+,\\theta^-)\\in\\Theta_H^2:\n O_H(\\theta^+)=O_H(\\theta^-),\\ \n T_{a,H}(\\theta^+)\\ne T_{a,H}(\\theta^-)\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nTaking Cartesian squares in (2) and using (3) gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}\n \\subseteq\\Theta_H^2\n \\subseteq\\mathfrak M_{\\mathrm{LGS}}(G^+)^2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(\\textnormal{thm:certificate-soundness}\\) implies that every verifier-accepted negative certificate lies in the set on the right side of (3); an unverified failure tag does not.\n\nNow let \\(w=(\\theta^+,\\theta^-)\\in\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{LGS}}^{(5)}\\).  If either coordinate assigns positive selector mass to some \\(s_0\\notin H\\), then that coordinate is outside \\(\\Theta_H\\) by \\(\\textnormal{ass:exact-selector-support}\\), so (3) excludes \\(w\\).  If instead some \\(c_0\\in\\mathcal C_H^{\\mathrm{adm}}(G^+)\\) has \\(p_{\\theta^\\varepsilon}(c_0)=0\\) for \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{+,-\\}\\), then \\(\\textnormal{ass:admissible-cell-interior}\\) again puts that coordinate outside \\(\\Theta_H\\).  Finally, if equality fails on any coordinate of \\(O_H\\), the equality required in (3) fails directly.  Each listed defect therefore implies\n\\[\n w\\notin\\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe definition of \\(\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{LGS}}^{(5)}\\) does not itself impose any of these three additional fixed-support obligations.  Hence membership in the published failure relation alone does not imply membership in \\(\\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}\\).  If the published relation contains a pair with one of the defects, (5) supplies an element of\n\\(\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{LGS}}^{(5)}\\setminus\\mathcal W_H^{\\mathrm{global}}\\), proving the final noninclusion.",
